<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :v3_overview %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:v3_overview) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The v3 Color Foundation') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            3.2 builds on the color system introduced in v3.0: 10 hues with 14 lightness steps, semantic roles (primary, success, error, warning), and a 14-step grayscale. Every color adapts to the device's palette and bit depth on its own, with no per-device markup. 3.1 added the TRMNL font bundle as the low-density default. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :colors) %> for the full palette.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Independent Text Scale') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Text Scale resizes all framework text without touching the rest of the interface. It stacks with Scale, so a dense layout can keep its text large instead of shrinking both together. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :text_scale) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Themes') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Recolor a whole screen by loading one extra stylesheet and adding <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--theme-&lt;id&gt;</code>. The theme picks the colors and the device still decides how they print: dither patterns on 1-bit, solids on full color. 3.2 ships three themes: Black and Yellow, White and Red, and Dark.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <li>A theme already decides every color, so <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--dark-mode</code> has no effect on a themed screen.</li>
            <li>Author your own theme with the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">theme-slots</code> mixins and validate it with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">rake framework:themes:lint</code>.</li>
            <li>Preview any docs example with a theme via the Style selector in the screen picker.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Full reference: <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :themes) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'TRMNLPaint: the JavaScript Paint API') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Read any framework color, border, text style, or size from JavaScript. <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint</code> returns what CSS would paint right now, with the device, scale, dark mode, and theme already applied, so your JavaScript never hardcodes a color.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <li>Resolvers: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bg()</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text()</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic()</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">series()</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border()</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">type()</code>, and more.</li>
            <li>Painters: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">apply()</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">applyBorder()</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">applyType()</code> write resolved paint onto nodes.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">watch()</code> re-runs your build function whenever the screen's device, scale, mode, dark mode, or theme changes.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">cssVar()</code> reads any public <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--*</code> variable, which is how a theme hands values to plugin code.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">scale()</code> reads the current scale factor, and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">px()</code> converts pixel numbers with it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Full reference: <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-2">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Generated Paint Assets') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Dither patterns no longer ship as PNG images. Every pattern is generated at build time as an
            inline SVG: the dark pixels live in the asset, the light tone comes from the background color,
            and a screen paints with zero image fetches.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <li>CSS and JavaScript share the same generated patterns: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint</code> reads back exactly what CSS paints.</li>
            <li>Dark mode and Raw/Preview swap in other patterns from the same generated set; the pattern shapes never fork.</li>
            <li>Regenerate from the color manifest with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">rake framework:color_tokens</code>; hand-edited pattern assets are gone.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Adaptive Charts') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Charts used to carry their own fixed colors and ignore bit depth, dark mode, and themes. The runtime now bundles <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLCharts</code>, a Highcharts adapter built on TRMNLPaint. Series colors, grid lines, and chart text all come from the framework, so a chart adapts with the rest of the screen.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">options()</code> and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">merge()</code> give you adaptive Highcharts defaults under your own settings.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">series(i, n)</code> picks each series color; <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">applySwatches()</code> paints matching legend markers.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">watch()</code> rebuilds the chart on device, scale, mode, dark mode, or theme changes.</li>
            <li><code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.px()</code> scales numeric chart dimensions from the same live CSS contract.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Usage and live examples: <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :chart) %>. For charting beyond Highcharts, use <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %> directly.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Adaptive Icons') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Add <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">image--adaptive</code> to a monochrome icon and the framework recolors it the way it colors text: it follows bit depth, dark mode, Raw/Preview, and the active theme. One set of icons works everywhere, as long as they are same-origin or CORS-readable. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :image) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Rebuilt Borders, Outlines, and Strokes') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The Border, Outline, Text Stroke, and Image Stroke utilities were rebuilt the same way as the color system. Each one now follows bit depth, dark mode, and themes on its own, instead of carrying fixed colors.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <li><strong>Borders:</strong> <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border--h-{step}</code> and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border--v-{step}</code> now use the same 10 to 75 shade scale as backgrounds, plus <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border--h-black</code> and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border--h-white</code>. Lines render as generated gradients instead of PNG tiles, so a bordered screen fetches no images and themes recolor it freely. The numbered levels <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border--h-1</code> through <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border--h-7</code> still work but are deprecated. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :border) %>.</li>
            <li><strong>Outline:</strong> draws a pixel-perfect dotted rounded border on 1-bit, and a solid rounded border on 2-bit and 4-bit. Dark mode and themes recolor it. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :outline) %>.</li>
            <li><strong>Text Stroke:</strong> renders as stacked drop shadows instead of native <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">-webkit-text-stroke</code>, so the stroke sits behind the letters in every browser, even when the text is filled with a pattern. Sizes run <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-stroke--small</code> to <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-stroke--xlarge</code>; dark mode and themes recolor them. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :text_stroke) %>.</li>
            <li><strong>Image Stroke:</strong> outlines transparent images with the same size and color variants; themed and dark-mode screens recolor them too. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :image_stroke) %>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Mashup Backdrop') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--backdrop</code> modifier now follows themes and dark mode like every other surface. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :mashup) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Fluid Mashups') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Fluid Mashups sit alongside the fixed layouts: place views on a three by three grid with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">mashup--3x3</code> and the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">mashup-cell</code> modifiers, no inline styles needed. Every cell keeps its frame and a compact title bar at any size, on every device, in dark mode, and under themes. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :mashup) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Complete Scale Modifiers') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Scale modifiers now resize everything: gaps, pixel utilities, image presets, component geometry, radii, and strokes, on top of each device's own density. The new <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--scale-xxsmall</code> level adds a 66% scale for dense mashups, and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.px()</code> lets JavaScript convert pixel numbers the same way. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :scale) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Under the Hood') %>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <li><strong>Smaller stylesheets:</strong> the responsive and device rules were refactored onto <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">:is()</code> selectors, shrinking the CSS.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Start Here') %>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <li>Upgrading an existing plugin? → <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :v3_upgrade_guide) %>.</li>
            <li>Want to adopt themes, adaptive charts, and adaptive icons? → <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :v3_enhancement_guide) %>.</li>
            <li>New to the framework? Start with <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :colors) %> and <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :themes) %>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
